<?xml version='1.0' encoding='UTF-8'?>
<preface xml:id="grv-preface">
  <title>Preface</title>
  <p>The Grothendieck ring of varieties records algebraic varieties through cutting and Cartesian product. Its defining relation is
    <md number="no">[X]=[Z]+[X\setminus Z],</md>
    for a closed subvariety <m>Z\subseteq X</m>. Deciding whether a class vanishes, whether multiplication by a variety can be cancelled, or whether a generating series is rational can require substantial geometry. These notes ask how much the ring remembers, how its zero divisors arise, and what is gained by inverting the affine-line class <m>\LL=[\A^1]</m>.</p>
  <p>Three results organize the discussion. In characteristic zero, Bittner's theorem presents the ring using smooth projective varieties and blowups. The Larsen–Lunts theorem identifies its quotient by <m>\LL</m> with the monoid ring of stable birational classes. Yet <m>\LL</m> is a zero divisor: there are smooth projective Calabi–Yau threefolds <m>X,Y</m> with
    <md number="no">\LL([X]-[Y])=0,\qquad [X]-[Y]\notin(\LL).</md>
    Thus reduction modulo <m>\LL</m> and inversion of <m>\LL</m> reveal different information. Equality after inversion is called <m>\LL</m>-equivalence.</p>
  <p>Unless a statement specifies another field, <m>k</m> is algebraically closed of characteristic zero. Varieties are reduced separated schemes of finite type; they may be singular, reducible, or nonproper. <xref ref="grv-ch-scissors"/> explains why several changes in these conventions give the same ring and why other changes require different relations. The roles of motives and of the ground field receive their own chapters within these notes.</p>
  <p>Motives enter through the same blowup formulas that present the ring: in characteristic zero there is a motivic Euler characteristic sending a smooth projective variety to the class of its rational Chow motive. Its target has direct sum relations, so equality there gives stable additive isomorphism and does not alone give an isomorphism of motives. Nonisomorphic isogenous elliptic curves already have isomorphic rational Chow motives and distinct variety classes. Integral realizations retain further information. These distinctions explain both the usefulness and the limits of a motivic test for <m>\LL</m>-equivalence.</p>
  <p>Changing the ground field introduces a different comparison. If <m>k</m> is algebraically closed, in any characteristic, then <m>K_0(\Var_k)\to K_0(\Var_K)</m> is injective for every extension <m>K/k</m>; the proof spreads a finite scissors identity and specializes it to a <m>k</m>-point. The same finite-data method, applied through a countable field of definition and a finite cover, gives a uniform annihilating exponent for smooth projective complex families whose fibers are all <m>\LL</m>-equivalent. These transfer statements do not identify the rings: in characteristic zero their cardinalities distinguish <m>\overline{\Q}</m> from <m>\C</m>. In positive characteristic, smooth stratifications remain available, while the characteristic-zero presentation theorems require their stated hypotheses. Universal homeomorphisms also lead to a modified ring whose comparison with the ordinary ring must be treated separately.</p>
  <p xml:id="grv-app-inputs">The prerequisites are basic algebraic geometry, including projective bundles, blowups, and birational maps, together with elementary commutative algebra. The motives chapter assumes Chow groups and basic additive-category language. Resolution, weak factorization, Hodge theory, the maximal rationally connected quotient, and derived categories enter through precise external statements. The formal properties of Chow motives, compact support motives, and integral Hodge realizations are likewise stated as black boxes with the exact output used. Elementary ring arguments, the base-change proof, and representative geometric calculations are developed; the arithmetic construction of abelian varieties, homogeneous-space constructions, and deeper irrationality theorems are cited with source locators.</p>
  <p>Begin with <xref ref="grv-ch-scissors"/>, <xref ref="grv-ch-birational"/>, and <xref ref="grv-ch-zero-divisors"/>. These chapters include an explicit elliptic curve whose class is a zero divisor, as well as the affine-line example. <xref ref="grv-ch-l-equivalence"/> separates localization from geometric constructions and comparisons with derived equivalence. <xref ref="grv-ch-symmetric"/> begins with the curve calculation and distinguishes rationality notions before stating the surface and higher-dimensional results. <xref ref="grv-ch-motives"/> develops the realizations used to compare classes and explains rationality after passage to motives. <xref ref="grv-ch-fields"/> proves the field-extension statements and treats positive characteristic. <xref ref="grv-ch-questions"/> gathers further problems, distinguishing attributed conjectures from questions proposed for study. <xref ref="grv-app-comparisons"/> proves the birational-class description of dimension-truncated scissors groups and exhibits a higher-dimensional relation that disappears in the full ring.</p>
  <p>Solid arrows indicate main inputs; dashed arrows indicate local inputs.</p>
  <image xml:id="grv-structure-map" source="images/grv-structure-map.svg" width="100%">
    <description><p>Selected dependencies among the eight chapters. Solid arrows indicate main inputs, and dashed arrows indicate local inputs. The branches connect scissors relations to fields, blowups to motives, and symmetric powers and fields to Further problems.</p></description>
  </image>
  <p>For background and systematic foundations, the principal reference is Chambert-Loir–Nicaise–Sebag <xref ref="grv-bib-CLNS"/>. Mustaţă <xref ref="grv-bib-Mustata"/> relates symmetric products to point counting and develops the early nonrationality argument for surfaces. Bittner <xref ref="grv-bib-Bittner"/>, Larsen–Lunts <xref ref="grv-bib-LL03"/> and <xref ref="grv-bib-LL04"/>, and Liu–Sebag <xref ref="grv-bib-LiuSebag"/> provide the central presentations, birational tests, and generating-series questions. Poonen, Borisov, Ito–Miura–Okawa–Ueda, and Ekedahl supply different nonvanishing and zero-divisor mechanisms. Nicaise and Nicaise–Sebag supply the spreading formalism and the modified ring. Each chapter identifies the source versions, hypotheses, and conclusions actually used. The recent positive-characteristic separation result is explicitly conditional on weak resolution and is recorded as a preprint claim.</p>
  <p xml:id="grm-reading-further">For the class calculations, the Chow-motive constructions and blowup formulas in <xref ref="grm-chow"/> supply the required formalism. Efimov <xref ref="grv-bib-Efimov"/> gives integral obstructions to localized equality, while André <xref ref="grm-bib-andre"/> compares variety-valued and motive-valued zeta functions. Studying isomorphisms of individual motives requires correspondences and their composition; Scholl <xref ref="grm-bib-scholl"/> develops that theory for curves and abelian varieties. For singular or nonproper varieties, the compact support triangle leads to Euler classes, weight complexes, and geometric motives. Gillet–Soulé <xref ref="grm-bib-gillet-soule"/> treat weight complexes, while Mazza–Voevodsky–Weibel <xref ref="grm-bib-mvw"/> and the references in Tabuada <xref ref="grm-bib-tabuada"/> provide the construction and descent theory beyond the formal properties used here.</p><p>These are working study notes, under continuing review. The literature and question statements were checked for this edition on 28 October 2026; that date is not a guarantee of completeness. Recent preprint results are identified as such. AI assistance was used in drafting, organizing, and checking the exposition and references. The source citations and the distinction between proved statements, attributed conjectures, and questions proposed for study are intended to make subsequent correction straightforward.</p>
</preface>
